On Mar 30, 2010, at 11:34, Michael Everson wrote:

>> The right-side, I can deal with most of it. There is one thing in =20
>> that example that is seriously grating, however. To use the first =20
>> sentence: "...gi'e zukte fi noda. I abuboi...." This is horrible. =20
>> Accepting your desire to capitalize for this example only, that =20
>> phrase should be "...gie' zukte fi noda .i Abuboi....".
>
> So you're treating .i like a danda, or | not belonging to either =20
> sentence? What do people think about that?


I don't recall what the formal grammar has to say (if it does; the =20
grammar is constructed largely without regard to semantics but only =20
accept/reject), but for example, free modifiers (such as =93za'a=94) =20
attach to the construct whose =93head=94, shall we say, is the preceding =
=20
word. In =93broda i za'a brode=94, the =93za'a=94 is considered to apply to=
 =20
the sentence =93brode=94, not =93broda=94. Therefore =93i=94 is associated =
with =20
the *following* sentence, not the preceding ones.

However, I almost agree with the notion of capitalizing that-after-=20
the-=93i=94, rather than the =93i=94 on *aesthetic* grounds, of not =20
capitalizing the same letter all the time.

In the case of connectives, =93i Ja abuboi=94 feels very wrong to me, as =
=20
opposed to =93I ja abuboi=94 or =93i ja Abuboi=94.

(Note that this is written in the context of =93If we capitalize, where =20
do we put it=94, without necessarily approving of capitalization at all.)

